    "turn_text": "past, present, and future, thereby diverting us from an understanding of human things that unfold in time, including the paths of our own lives. Now enter new searches for meanings. Now enter deaths and diseases of despair. Now enter a whole generation of students who prefer the model, the mode of governance we defeated 76 years ago. Socialism. That's a turn. It's all a turn, and it's for the worst. How do you continue the most advanced set of human relations the world has ever known when you want to ape and emulate what we used to think of as a force for misery and evil? Leo Strauss, the great political philosopher, saw this coming when he spoke about it as far long ago as 1949, only four short years after World War II ended. He put it this way. It would not be the first time that a nation defeated on the battlefield, and as it were, annihilated as a political being, has deprived its conqueror of the most sublime fruit of victory by imposing on him the yoke of its own thought. Whatever might be true of the thought of the American people, American social science has adopted the very attitude toward neutral right, which a generation ago could still be described with some plausibility as German relativism. The majority among the learned who still cherish the principles of the Declaration of Independence interpret these principles not as formulations of natural right, but as an ideal, if not an ideology or a myth. Present-day American social science is dedicated to the proposition that all men are endowed by the evolutionary process, or by a mysterious fate, with all kinds of urges and aspirations, but certainly not with unalienable rights. Think again, if you will, about what we quoted Woodrow Wilson as saying about the Declaration of Independence. Woodrow Wilson, the founder, really, of the progressive movement in America. He said, if you want to understand the real Declaration of Independence, do not repeat the preface. Make a new table of contents, make a new set of counts in the indictment, make a new statement of the things you mean to set right, and then call all the civilized world to witness. We are not bound to adhere to the doctrines held by the signers of the Declaration of Independence. We are as free as they were to make and unmake governments. We are not here to worship men or a document. It is no fetish. Its words lay no compulsion upon the thought of any free man.\" Can you imagine Jefferson or Madison or Franklin ever thinking that they were writing so that they would be interpreted that way? Thank God the boys at Point of Hawk didn't see it that way, and thank God for an America that didn't used to see it that way. The question is, can we summon up what we once had and go back as if full circle to Ronald Reagan's greatest wish, a new birth of freedom based on an informed patriotism? Or will rather the modern age leave us in the place where patriotism remains a down market commodity and we, like so many animals other than rational, jump from lily pad to lily pad of life into a meaningless unrelated rush of events? That, as the national anthem is a question, is also our major question today. I'm Seth Leibson. We'll be right back. welcome back to the set leaps in show happy friday again open lines friday six zero two five zero eight zero nine six zero anything on your mind feel free to bring it up a little more loose about uh... topics if you uh... someone who'd been on hold earlier in the week yesterday didn't make it on let us know we'll put you right to the top i was some talk to a friend of mine who uh... is in the think tank world has been for a long time back east And I was reminiscing with him a little bit about the situation we're now looking at the riots, the protests, urban civilization and its discontents, if you will. We were talking about something interesting in looking at it from all these problems from a conservative point of view or a conservative perspective, if you will. And he reminded me of something I knew I just hadn't thought about in a long time, although we may have mentioned it here once or twice, which is, you know, that is really these kinds of issues, the riots in the 60s, were what really gave birth. to a movement that has come to have a disparaging name, which is neo conservatism. A lot of people today think of neo conservatism or dismiss neo conservatism as a ideology of foreign policy within the conservative movement of the Republican Party. But it started out, literally started out over these issues, over inner city and urban issues. That's what Irving Crystal was a professor of Bill Crystal's dad. He was a professor of urban policy. James Q. Wilson, who was mentioned by Heather Macdonald yesterday at Harvard. who gave us the Broken Windows theory that was so much responsible for the cleaning up of New York when it finally came to be deployed by Bratton, William Bratton. This is where all those people cut their teeth. Norman put Horitz, editor of commentary in the 60s and early 70s, was mostly publishing articles on issues having to do with urban affairs and inner city crime and poverty. Daniel Moynihan who considered himself a neoconservative, though a Democrat, that was his expertise and specialty. You may recall he wrote that famous report for Lyndon Johnson on the fate of the American family known as the Moynihan report. It was just kind of something that we were thinking about, because we kind of missed that. We don't have a lot of these scholars around anymore. They're from a generation that has come past. Heather certainly is one of them. Heather McDonald would certainly probably be the dean of that school, but it's not a very large faculty. She's dean of. There aren't too many, too terribly many people. who are still entrenched in those areas of study and discussion and writing, which is really very unfortunate because the history is fascinating and we've been here before. We've been here before in the 1960s, particularly starting in 1967 with the riots in Detroit and then, of course, 1968 with the riots that came around after the assassination of Martin Luther King. We have been here before and the thing that's so sad to me about public policy and it's the frustration of those who dedicate their lives to taking it seriously is that there's almost it's almost as if there's no learning there's a lot of teaching it's Not the problem that there's not enough teaching. It's a problem that there's not enough learning. If we've been here before, you would think we would avoid repeating it again. And one is inclined to bring up the George Santana Yana quote that those who refused to learn from history or destined to repeat it. An overused line, but overused precisely because it is so true. That's what's sad to me here. It's sad to me that the learning has gone in the other direction. Junk thought, that's the phrase I've been using a lot lately. Junk thought has overtaken and overcome some of the successes we were on the precipice of achieving. Give you a modern day example. we right now saw a tragic and terrible horrible you pick the adjective you want there isn't one dark enough to describe the death of George Floyd and it in some respects seemed to be the spark or was indeed the spark that let off a lot of these fires It would not be the first time that riots in the street came from a spark no one predicted. Indeed, most of the riots we've lived through before in this country came from sparks no one would have predicted. The question then becomes, how do you build a community after its rioting has destroyed it so that it doesn't have to happen again, given the grievance? In the 60s, the grievances, believe it or not, weren't as much racial as they were economic. that we seemed to be on the way to fixing. We seemed to be getting more and more great numbers for our minority communities as far as moving them into the middle class, as far as moving them from the middle class into the upper classes, into the top quintiles of socio-economic status. We seem to be moving in a very good direction and specially especially over the last three years, although not beginning in the last three years. I mean, it started to really move in the 80s and it's been a constant move upward, but it took a dramatic shift upward over the last three years. So having mostly gotten the economic part of this right, which was so much the gripe of the 60s, It's odd that it's now redounded back to a racial gripe, isn't it? That's what's so sad to me is we thought we had a handle. We thought we had a handle with the Civil Rights Act and with desegregation, and even with objectionable policies if you want to call them that as I would, but nonetheless policies that dominated our socio-economic structures of affirmative action and racial preferences, we thought we had the race problem solved. When we elected Barack Obama as a president so many thought, so many thought and indeed I even knew Republicans who voted for him for the very purpose of saying let us get beyond once and for all the rate the sad fact of race of racism in America and prove that once and for all a black person yes can grow up to be anything he or she wants including president of the United States and not just a governor not just a mayor not just a US senator but president of the United States indeed We thought we did that. We thought we solved it. Only to find ourselves where we are today here. Why? In a sense. Collective guilt is what's inspiring it. The protesters are not the majorities of Americans. The rioters are not the majorities of America. But there are ever large numbers of Americans who are joining their cry out of a sense of collective responsibility in guilt. That I think is a huge problem and I want to talk about it when we come back. I'm Seth, 602-508-0960. We'll be right back. Welcome back to the Seth leaps and show I am Seth yes June 25th we have this great event with Dennis Prager and we grew to morality in the 21st century how have feelings become so much more important than truth and virtue. Can we communicate our self-evident truths that we have come to rely on and know since our founding to the younger generations? More information and tickets available at 960thepatriot.com. I'll be there. It'd be great to see you there. George is in Phoenix. Hello, George. George, are you there?",
